{
  "slug": "artist:u2",
  "name": "U2",
  "editorial_article": "U2 are an iconic Irish rock band formed in Dublin in 1976 that became one of the most commercially successful and influential musical acts in history. Comprising Bono (vocals), The Edge (guitar, keyboards, vocals), Adam Clayton (bass), and Larry Mullen Jr. (drums), the group’s sound evolved from post-punk roots into a global, anthemic rock phenomenon. Throughout their career, they have consistently redefined their musical identity, moving from the political intensity of the early 1980s—marked by the breakout success of the album 'War'—to the expansive, atmospheric textures of 'The Joshua Tree' (1987), which solidified their status as stadium-filling superstars.\n\nIn the 1990s, the band underwent a radical artistic reinvention, embracing irony, industrial music, and electronic dance music on 'Achtung Baby' (1991) and the ambitious 'Zoo TV' tour. This period proved they were not content with past triumphs, constantly challenging both themselves and their audience. Producers like Brian Eno and Daniel Lanois were instrumental in crafting their signature, reverb-drenched sonic landscapes, particularly the Edge's unique, delay-heavy guitar style. Beyond their musical output, U2 is deeply integrated into global culture through their long-term social activism, campaigning on human rights, debt relief, and global health, solidifying a legacy defined by both massive commercial success and a persistent, often polarizing, commitment to high-minded thematic exploration.",
